WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:03.429
Which scene in The Batman is giving audiences
"chills?"

00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:06.850
Even Robert Pattinson was caught off guard
by this scene – plus, a behind-the-scenes

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.410
look at how the detective elements came about.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:10.709
All that and more, right here!

00:00:10.709 --> 00:00:12.780
By and away, people seem to love "The Batman."

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.180
With Robert Pattinson in the mask, the Caped
Crusader's latest solo adventure sees him

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.160
responding to a series of murders perpetrated
by a villain calling himself Riddler.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.400
To solve the crimes and prevent anyone else
from dying, Batman will have to descend into

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.661
the seedy underbelly of Gotham to uncover
the truth, unraveling a conspiracy in the

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:32.000
process that threatens everything he once
believed about the city.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.010
From Batman beating up a group of thugs harassing
an innocent man to the thrilling car chase

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:39.969
involving Batman and Penguin, there's plenty
to sink your teeth into.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:43.309
And naturally, fans have taken to social media
to voice their love and admiration toward

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:44.970
the film, hyping it up.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.960
One scene in particular appears to have stoked
fans' excitement.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.000
And the good news is that it comes early in
the film's run.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.110
It's become commonplace in Batman movies for
the opening scene to revolve around the villain.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:58.720
For "The Dark Knight," that involved a thrilling
heist involving Joker and a few other nobodies

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.610
wanting to steal the mob's money.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.640
In "The Dark Knight Rises," director Christopher
Nolan upped the ante yet again by introducing

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.880
viewers to Bane in high-flying fashion as
he hijacks a plane.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.720
"The Batman" carries on this tradition by
opening the film with Riddler spying on the

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.110
home of Gotham City mayor Don Mitchell Jr.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.299
It then cuts to Riddler inside of his house,
ready to strike and instill fears into the

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:20.000
hearts of Gothamites.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.369
It's incredibly effective at establishing
the film's tone and displaying how much of

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:24.970
a threat Riddler is.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:28.890
This isn't just a guy telling riddles as he's
been portrayed in other Batman media.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:32.640
He's a terrifying serial killer, to the point
where the scene feels like something out of

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.680
"Zodiac" than a superhero movie.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.040
It's proven to be so effective that numerous
people have taken to social media to say how

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.880
much they loved the sequence.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:40.939
One Twitter user wrote:

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:45.259
"The opening scene is so powerful and one
of my favorite scenes in all of film."

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:48.380
Those sentiments were echoed by another user,
who had this to say,

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.930
"Can't stop thinking about #TheBatman and
THAT opening scene.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.780
Definitely not gonna be able to sleep much
tonight."

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.509
It seems as if the impact of the scene isn't
lost upon later views, either.

00:01:58.509 --> 00:01:59.630
As a third user wrote:

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:00.740
"That opening man.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.600
2nd time seeing it and it still sends chills."

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.920
It isn't just casual fans who are being taken
aback by "The Batman" opening scene either.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.770
Prior to the film's release, Robert Pattinson
spoke with Variety about the movie, and even

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:15.110
he admits that when he watched the sequence
for the first time, it was unlike anything

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.380
he had seen before.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:17.380
The actor mentioned,

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.660
"I watched a rough cut of the movie by myself.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.860
And the first shot is so jarring from any
other Batman movie that it's just kind of

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.330
a totally different pace."

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.770
Pattinson went on to say that the tone of
that scene was pretty much what director Matt

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.080
Reeves had envisioned doing from the very
beginning:

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.470
"It was what Matt was saying from the first
meeting I had with him: 'I want to do a '70s

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.930
noir detective story, like 'The Conversation.’

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:40.300
And I kind of assumed that meant the mood
board or something, the look of it.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.050
But from the first shot, it's, 'Oh, this actually
is a detective story.'"

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:48.410
It may take some viewers by surprise to see
Batman in full noir mode, but he's the world's

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.280
greatest detective for a reason.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.920
And that reason is something Pattinson only
learned after he nabbed the part:

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.540
"There's a lot of stuff where he's in amongst
the cops.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.300
Normally, when you see Batman he arrives and
beats people up.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.270
But he's having conversations, and there are
emotional scenes between them, which I don't

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.470
think have been in any of the other movies."

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:09.620
Indeed, from the opening scene to the end
credits, "The Batman" is unlike any Caped

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:14.400
Crusader movie that's come before, and it
sets a new bar for the future of the franchise.

